// std/diag.jtr — structured diagnostics: a source map, spans, labels, and a
// renderer that does not own its destination.
//
// TIER: building a `SourceMap` or a `Diagnostic` allocates (`@no_os`, not
// `@no_alloc`); **rendering does neither** (`@no_alloc @no_os`). That split is the
// module's shape in one line: the data structure is built once with an allocator you
// hand it, and every rendering afterwards is a pure function into a buffer you own.
//
// ─── Why there is no `Writer` parameter ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// The obvious signature is `render(w: file.Writer, …) -> usize !{ RenderFailed }`,
// and it is wrong twice over.
//
// **The error set would be a lie.** `sink` writes are infallible at the call site by
// deliberate design: a byte that does not fit is COUNTED, not returned, so a formatter
// emitting a hundred pieces carries zero `?`s. And `file.Writer`'s one fallible call is
// `finish`. A renderer between them has nothing to fail at — an error set there would be
// `writer.jtr`'s deleted `failed()` in a new costume: a query that can only ever answer
// "fine" is worse than no query, because it invites a caller to believe something was
// checked.
//
// **The writer parameter would cost a second buffer.** `writer.Writer`'s stream targets
// are LINE-oriented (the print intrinsics append their own terminator) and already carry
// a `Sink` on every call, so a writer-taking renderer would need one sink to assemble a
// line and a second to be written into. Rendering into a caller `Sink` composes with all
// three destinations with no such doubling:
//
//     diag.render(s, buf, m, d, th)
//     if sink.overflowed(s) { … }                      // ← NOT optional; see below
//     eprint_str(sink.view(s, buf))                    // stderr
//     file.write_str(w, sink.view(s, buf))             // a file
//     writer.put_str(w, s2, b2, sink.view(s, buf))     // the trait
//
// So the output target IS explicit — it is simply the caller's, which is the same
// answer `sink` and `test`'s `Check` already arrived at, and it keeps this module free
// of `io`, `file` and `writer` and therefore genuinely `@no_os`.
//
// ─── The overflow contract, which is the one thing a caller must not skip ─────
//
// A diagnostic is not bounded: a long source line and a long message can exceed any
// fixed buffer. When that happens `render` returns the number of bytes it MANAGED to
// write and the sink counts the rest as lost. **Every oracle then answers truthfully and
// the output is still corrupt** — `render` reports what it wrote (it did), a following
// `file.write_str` reports the prefix as fully written (it was), `finish` reports the
// stream fine (it was), and what lands on disk is a diagnostic cut in half, quite
// possibly mid-caret. The only signal is `sink.overflowed(s)`, so it belongs in the
// recipe above rather than in a footnote. `diag_test.jtr` performs the unguarded render
// on purpose so the suite shows what a caller gets without the guard.
//
// ─── Storage: two arenas, no per-label ownership ──────────────────────────────
//
// A `SourceMap` is every file's text concatenated into ONE `String`, every file's name
// concatenated into another, and a `List(i32)` holding four offsets per file. A
// `Diagnostic` is the same shape at a smaller scale: one `String` arena holding the
// message, the code and every label's text, with each `Label` storing a slice into it.
//
// That is not cleverness, it is the only shape available. A Jestyr borrow is
// second-class, so a `read str` cannot be stored in a struct that outlives the call; and
// a `List(String)` would leak, because B1's field auto-drop recurses into droppable
// FIELDS and `String` is a primitive with a manual `string_free` — the trap `std/pathbuf`
// paid for. Concatenating sidesteps both: there is exactly one owned buffer per arena and
// exactly one `string_free` for it.
//
// Measured while building this, and worth knowing before you write any `Drop` impl over a
// mixed struct: **an explicit `impl Drop` does NOT replace the field auto-drop, it runs
// before it.** The emitted C for `SourceMap` calls `Drop__SourceMap__drop` (the two
// `String`s) and then `Drop__List_i32___drop` on the `ix` field. So an impl covering the
// primitives is right and adding `list.free` to it would be a double free.
//
// ─── What a `Label` is, and why `Note` and `Suggestion` are not separate types ─
//
// The API has four concepts — primary label, secondary label, note, suggestion — and ONE
// record. They differ in exactly two bits of behaviour: whether they point at source, and
// which character underlines it. Three structs that agree on all their fields would be
// three ways to store one thing, which the renderer would then have to walk three times
// in the right interleaving to keep the caller's order. One record with a `LabelKind`
// keeps the order for free and keeps `render` a single loop.
//
// ─── The COLUMN is bytes; the CARET is cells. They are different questions ────
//
// This header used to say both were byte-based and that the pin below was "the test to
// invert when a display-width function exists". `std/width` now exists, and **the pin did
// not invert** — because measuring the two in the same units was never the goal, and doing
// it would have made one of them wrong:
//
//   * **The caret is ALIGNMENT.** It has to land under its token in a terminal, so it is
//     measured in display cells: a CJK ideograph is three bytes and two cells, a combining
//     mark is two bytes and none. `expanded_width` asks `std/width` for every non-tab
//     character, and the underline is the difference of two such measurements so it cannot
//     disagree with the indent above it.
//
//   * **The reported column is NAVIGATION.** `--> file:1:3` is a coordinate a person pastes
//     into an editor and a coordinate `jestyrc --json` also emits
//     (`docs/diagnostics-json.md` documents `col` as 1-based with no character claim). A
//     tool consuming that JSON to place a marker in a buffer needs an offset into the TEXT,
//     and a terminal-cell count is not one. So `pos_of` still counts bytes, and the renderer
//     and the JSON still agree about where a diagnostic points — which is the property the
//     old note was actually protecting.
//
// Stating it as one decision was the error; they are two, and they now have two answers.
// `caret_alignment_is_byte_based_and_tabs_expand` keeps the byte-column half and
// `a_wide_character_moves_the_caret_two_cells` pins the other, so a future change that
// merges them has to break one on purpose.
//
// TABS stay this module's business rather than `std/width`'s, because a tab's width depends
// on the COLUMN it starts at and not on the character: it expands to `th.tab_width` in BOTH
// the source row and the caret row, and `std/width` answers 0 for one rather than guessing.
//
// Run the tests:  jestyrc test examples/std/diag_test.jtr
// Worked example: jestyrc run examples/std/diag_demo.jtr examples/std/diag_demo.jtr

import "list"
import "width"
import "sink"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ identifiers

// A file's index in a `SourceMap`.
//
// `distinct` rather than a bare `usize` because the two numbers this module traffics in —
// a file index and a byte offset — are both `usize` and are never interchangeable, and
// mixing them silently reads the wrong file's text. The nominal type makes that a compile
// error; `distinct`'s operation inheritance is what makes the cost bearable, since a
// `FileId` still compares with `==` and still arithmetics with another `FileId`.
//
// The bill, measured rather than guessed (the `std/path` lesson: measure the conversion
// you would actually ship): **11 casts inside this module**, all at the boundary where an
// id becomes a table index, and **0 in `diag_demo.jtr`**, which never spells an id at all
// — it takes what `add_file` returns and hands it back. That distribution is the opposite
// of the one that shelved typed `Path` (111 of 132 casts on callers), which is what makes
// it worth having here.
pub distinct FileId = usize

// A byte range in one file. Offsets are FILE-RELATIVE, not relative to the map's
// concatenated text: a span stays meaningful if the map is later rebuilt with its files
// in a different order, and `pos_of` only has to scan the one file's slice. The price is
// that a span is meaningless without its `FileId`, which is why the field is not optional.
@copy pub struct Span {
    pub file: FileId,
    pub start: usize,
    pub end: usize,
}

// A 1-based line and BYTE column — the units `jestyrc --json` already reports.
@copy pub struct Pos {
    pub line: usize,
    pub col: usize,
}

pub fn span(f: FileId, start: usize, end: usize) -> Span {
    return Span{ file: f, start: start, end: end }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- source map

// Every file's source in one buffer, every file's name in another, and four offsets per
// file in `ix`: name_start, name_end, src_start, src_end. See the header on why this is
// concatenation rather than a `List(String)`.
pub struct SourceMap {
    text: String,
    names: String,
    ix: List(i32),
}

// The two `String`s are freed here; `ix` is freed by the field auto-drop that runs after
// this body. Do not add `list.free` — see the header.
impl Drop for SourceMap {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        string_free(self.text)
        string_free(self.names)
    }
}

pub fn map_new(read a: Allocator) -> SourceMap {
    return SourceMap{ text: string_new(), names: string_new(), ix: list.make(i32, a) }
}

// Register `src` under `name` and return its id. The map COPIES both, so the caller may
// free or reuse its own buffers immediately — which is what lets a driver read a file,
// hand it over, and drop the `String` it read into.
pub fn add_file(mut m: SourceMap, read name: str, read src: str) -> FileId {
    let id: usize = list.len(i32, m.ix) / 4
    let nv0: str = string_view(m.names)
    let ns: usize = nv0.len
    string_push(m.names, name)
    let nv1: str = string_view(m.names)
    let tv0: str = string_view(m.text)
    let ss: usize = tv0.len
    string_push(m.text, src)
    let tv1: str = string_view(m.text)
    list.push(i32, m.ix, ns as i32)
    list.push(i32, m.ix, nv1.len as i32)
    list.push(i32, m.ix, ss as i32)
    list.push(i32, m.ix, tv1.len as i32)
    return id as FileId
}

pub fn file_count(read m: SourceMap) -> usize {
    return list.len(i32, m.ix) / 4
}

// `true` if `f` names a file this map holds. Every accessor below is caller-checked
// against this, in the `list.get` tradition: a bounds-checked variant is future work for
// the whole tree, not a policy this module gets to invent alone.
pub fn has_file(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId) -> bool {
    return (f as usize) < file_count(m)
}

pub fn file_name(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId) -> str {
    let b: usize = (f as usize) * 4
    let nv: str = string_view(m.names)
    return nv[list.get(i32, m.ix, b) as usize .. list.get(i32, m.ix, b + 1) as usize]
}

pub fn file_source(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId) -> str {
    let b: usize = (f as usize) * 4
    let tv: str = string_view(m.text)
    return tv[list.get(i32, m.ix, b + 2) as usize .. list.get(i32, m.ix, b + 3) as usize]
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- line and column mapping

// The 1-based line and byte column of `off` in `f`. An offset past the end of the file
// clamps to the last position rather than reading out of bounds, because a diagnostic
// about a truncated file is exactly when a span runs off the end and a renderer that
// crashed there would lose the one report that mattered.
pub fn pos_of(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId, off: usize) -> Pos {
    let s: str = file_source(m, f)
    var lim: usize = off
    if lim > s.len { lim = s.len }
    var line: usize = 1
    var col: usize = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < lim {
        if (s[i] as i32) == 10 {
            line = line + 1
            col = 1
        } else {
            col = col + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return Pos{ line: line, col: col }
}

// How many lines `f` has. A file not ending in a newline still counts its last partial
// line; an empty file has one (empty) line, so `line_text(m, f, 1)` is always legal.
pub fn line_count(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId) -> usize {
    let s: str = file_source(m, f)
    var n: usize = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        if (s[i] as i32) == 10 {
            if i + 1 < s.len { n = n + 1 }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

// The text of 1-based `line`, WITHOUT its terminator. Out of range gives the empty
// string, which is what lets the context-line loop in `render` ask for lines that may not
// exist without a bounds dance at every call.
pub fn line_text(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId, line: usize) -> str {
    let s: str = file_source(m, f)
    if line == 0 { return s[0 .. 0] }
    var cur: usize = 1
    var start: usize = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        if (s[i] as i32) == 10 {
            if cur == line { return s[start .. i] }
            cur = cur + 1
            start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if cur == line { return s[start .. s.len] }
    return s[0 .. 0]
}

// The byte offset at which 1-based `line` starts. `render` needs this to turn a span's
// absolute offset into a column WITHIN the line it is underlining.
pub fn line_start(read m: SourceMap, f: FileId, line: usize) -> usize {
    let s: str = file_source(m, f)
    if line <= 1 { return 0 }
    var cur: usize = 1
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        if (s[i] as i32) == 10 {
            cur = cur + 1
            if cur == line { return i + 1 }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return s.len
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics

@copy pub enum Severity {
    sev_error,
    sev_warning,
    sev_note,
    sev_help,
}

// What a label does. `lab_primary` and `lab_secondary` point at source and underline it
// (`^` and `-` respectively, the convention every terminal reader already knows);
// `lab_note` and `lab_help` render as trailing `= note:` / `= help:` lines. A
// SUGGESTION is a `lab_help` that also carries a span — see `suggest`.
@copy pub enum LabelKind {
    lab_primary,
    lab_secondary,
    lab_note,
    lab_help,
}

// One annotation. `ts`/`te` slice this label's text out of the owning `Diagnostic`'s
// arena; `spanned` is false for a note that points at nothing in particular.
@copy pub struct Label {
    pub kind: LabelKind,
    pub span: Span,
    pub spanned: bool,
    ts: usize,
    te: usize,
}

// A diagnostic and everything it says. `arena` holds the message, the code and every
// label's text end to end; the `ms`/`me` and `cs`/`ce` pairs slice the first two out.
pub struct Diagnostic {
    pub sev: Severity,
    arena: String,
    ms: usize,
    me: usize,
    cs: usize,
    ce: usize,
    labels: List(Label),
}

// `arena` is freed here; `labels` by the field auto-drop that follows. See the header.
impl Drop for Diagnostic {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        string_free(self.arena)
    }
}

// A diagnostic with no labels yet. `code` may be empty, which renders as no `[…]` at all
// rather than as `[]` — a stable code is a promise to a consumer and an empty one is the
// honest way to say a pass has not made that promise yet.
pub fn make(read a: Allocator, sev: Severity, read code: str, read msg: str) -> Diagnostic {
    var arena: String = string_new()
    let v0: str = string_view(arena)
    let cs: usize = v0.len
    string_push(arena, code)
    let v1: str = string_view(arena)
    let ce: usize = v1.len
    string_push(arena, msg)
    let v2: str = string_view(arena)
    return Diagnostic{ sev: sev, arena: arena, ms: ce, me: v2.len, cs: cs, ce: ce, labels: list.make(Label, a) }
}

pub fn new_error(read a: Allocator, read code: str, read msg: str) -> Diagnostic {
    return make(a, sev_error, code, msg)
}

pub fn new_warning(read a: Allocator, read code: str, read msg: str) -> Diagnostic {
    return make(a, sev_warning, code, msg)
}

pub fn message(read d: Diagnostic) -> str {
    let v: str = string_view(d.arena)
    return v[d.ms .. d.me]
}

pub fn code(read d: Diagnostic) -> str {
    let v: str = string_view(d.arena)
    return v[d.cs .. d.ce]
}

pub fn label_count(read d: Diagnostic) -> usize {
    return list.len(Label, d.labels)
}

pub fn label_at(read d: Diagnostic, i: usize) -> Label {
    return list.get(Label, d.labels, i)
}

pub fn label_text(read d: Diagnostic, read l: Label) -> str {
    let v: str = string_view(d.arena)
    return v[l.ts .. l.te]
}

// The shared adder. Labels render in the order they were added — the caller's order, not
// a sort — for the same reason `--json` keeps emission order: a consumer that wants them
// sorted can sort, a consumer that wants to know what the pass said first cannot recover
// it once we have sorted here.
fn add_label(mut d: Diagnostic, k: LabelKind, sp: Span, spanned: bool, read text: str) {
    let v0: str = string_view(d.arena)
    let ts: usize = v0.len
    string_push(d.arena, text)
    let v1: str = string_view(d.arena)
    list.push(Label, d.labels, Label{ kind: k, span: sp, spanned: spanned, ts: ts, te: v1.len })
}

// The span this diagnostic is ABOUT. `text` may be empty, which underlines without
// annotating — right when the message above already said everything.
pub fn primary(mut d: Diagnostic, sp: Span, read text: str) {
    add_label(d, lab_primary, sp, true, text)
}

// A span that explains the primary one: the earlier binding, the trait that declared the
// signature, the first of the two conflicting definitions.
pub fn secondary(mut d: Diagnostic, sp: Span, read text: str) {
    add_label(d, lab_secondary, sp, true, text)
}

// A trailing `= note:` line, pointing at nothing.
pub fn note(mut d: Diagnostic, read text: str) {
    add_label(d, lab_note, span(0 as FileId, 0, 0), false, text)
}

// A trailing `= help:` line, pointing at nothing.
pub fn help(mut d: Diagnostic, read text: str) {
    add_label(d, lab_help, span(0 as FileId, 0, 0), false, text)
}

// A suggestion: a help that names the span it would REPLACE. Stored as a spanned
// `lab_help` so it renders under the code like a label rather than as a detached line —
// a suggestion whose location the reader has to reconstruct is not much of a suggestion.
//
// It deliberately does NOT apply itself, and there is no `apply` here. A machine-
// applicable rewrite has to say whether it is safe to apply unattended, and that is a
// data question for the machine renderer (`docs/diagnostics-json.md`'s `help` field is
// prose today), not something to guess from a string.
pub fn suggest(mut d: Diagnostic, sp: Span, read replacement: str) {
    add_label(d, lab_help, sp, true, replacement)
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------ theme

// Rendering policy, passed in rather than compiled in. `context_before`/`context_after`
// are source lines of surrounding context; `gutter` prints line numbers; `tab_width` is
// how far a tab expands in BOTH the source row and the caret row; `unicode` picks the
// vertical bar (`│` vs `|`) and nothing else, because `^` and `-` are not an ASCII
// fallback for anything — they are the convention.
@copy pub struct Theme {
    pub gutter: bool,
    pub tab_width: usize,
    pub unicode: bool,
    pub context_before: usize,
    pub context_after: usize,
}

// A theme with every knob given explicitly.
//
// This exists because a module-qualified struct LITERAL is a parse error —
// `diag.Theme{ gutter: false, … }` from another file does not parse, even though
// `diag.Theme` in type position is fine. So a `pub struct` a caller is expected to build
// needs an exported constructor, or it is effectively read-only outside its module. Worth
// knowing before designing any library type in this tree; found by writing the test that
// varies the theme, not by reading the grammar.
pub fn theme(gutter: bool, tab_width: usize, unicode: bool, context_before: usize, context_after: usize) -> Theme {
    return Theme{ gutter: gutter, tab_width: tab_width, unicode: unicode, context_before: context_before, context_after: context_after }
}

// ASCII, line numbers, no surrounding context. The default because it is the one that
// renders identically into a golden file, a CI log and a terminal that has lied about its
// encoding.
pub fn plain() -> Theme {
    return Theme{ gutter: true, tab_width: 4, unicode: false, context_before: 0, context_after: 0 }
}

// For a terminal: box-drawing bars and one line of context either side.
pub fn terminal() -> Theme {
    return Theme{ gutter: true, tab_width: 4, unicode: true, context_before: 1, context_after: 1 }
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- the renderer

const SP: i32 = 32
const NL: i32 = 10
const TAB: i32 = 9
const BAR: i32 = 124        // '|'
const EQ: i32 = 61          // '='
const CARET: i32 = 94       // '^'
const DASH: i32 = 45        // '-'

@no_alloc @no_os fn digits(n: usize) -> usize {
    var d: usize = 1
    var v: usize = n
    for v >= 10 {
        d = d + 1
        v = v / 10
    }
    return d
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn put_pad(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, n: usize) {
    for i in 0..n {
        sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
    }
}

// The vertical bar, honouring the theme. `│` is U+2502, three UTF-8 bytes — written as
// bytes rather than as a literal so this file stays pure ASCII and cannot be broken by an
// editor re-encoding it.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_bar(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read th: Theme) {
    if th.unicode {
        sink.put(s, buf, 226 as u8)
        sink.put(s, buf, 148 as u8)
        sink.put(s, buf, 130 as u8)
        return
    }
    sink.put(s, buf, BAR as u8)
}

// ` <pad> | ` — the row head with no line number.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_head_bar(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read th: Theme, w: usize) {
    put_pad(s, buf, w + 2)
    put_bar(s, buf, th)
}

// ` <lineno right-aligned in w> | ` — the row head that carries a line number.
//
// With `gutter` off it degrades to the numberless head. The first version merely set the
// width to zero and kept printing the number, which produced `2 | two` — a gutter with no
// column rather than no gutter, and the test that varied the theme is what caught it. A
// theme field named for what it shows has to be able to show nothing.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_head_num(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read th: Theme, w: usize, n: usize) {
    if th.gutter == false {
        put_head_bar(s, buf, th, w)
        sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
        return
    }
    sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
    let d: usize = digits(n)
    if w > d { put_pad(s, buf, w - d) }
    sink.put_usize(s, buf, n)
    sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
    put_bar(s, buf, th)
    sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
}

// Copy a source line with tabs expanded, and report the expanded width of the first
// `upto` BYTES of it — which is exactly the caret's column. One function does both so the
// two can never disagree about how wide a tab is, which is the whole failure mode.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_source(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read text: str, read th: Theme) {
    for i in 0..text.len {
        let c: i32 = text[i] as i32
        if c == TAB {
            put_pad(s, buf, th.tab_width)
        } else {
            sink.put(s, buf, text[i] as u8)
        }
    }
}

// The display width of the first `upto` BYTES, with tabs expanded.
//
// **Two different questions, and only one of them is this.** A tab is an ALIGNMENT question —
// its width depends on the theme, not on the character — so it is answered here. Everything
// else is a WIDTH question and is answered by `std/width`, which knows that a CJK ideograph
// is three bytes and two cells and that a combining mark is two bytes and none.
//
// Before this, every non-tab byte counted as one cell, and the header said so: a caret after
// any multi-byte character sat left of its token. That is now fixed for the CARET while the
// reported COLUMN stays byte-based — see the header on why those are not the same decision.
@no_alloc @no_os fn expanded_width(read text: str, upto: usize, read th: Theme) -> usize {
    var w: usize = 0
    var lim: usize = upto
    if lim > text.len { lim = text.len }
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < lim {
        if (text[i] as i32) == TAB {
            w = w + th.tab_width
            i = i + 1
        } else {
            var step: usize = width.seq_len(text, i)
            if step == 0 { step = 1 }
            w = w + width.width_upto(text[i .. i + step], step)
            i = i + step
        }
    }
    return w
}

// Enums carry no `Eq` in Jestyr, so every kind question goes through a `match`. Three
// total predicates rather than a comparison at each site: an exhaustive `match` is what
// makes adding a fifth `LabelKind` a compile error at each place that has to decide, and
// a chain of `==` would silently take the else branch instead.
@no_alloc @no_os fn under_char(k: LabelKind) -> i32 {
    match k {
        lab_primary => { return CARET },
        lab_secondary => { return DASH },
        lab_note => { return DASH },
        lab_help => { return DASH },
    }
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn is_primary(k: LabelKind) -> bool {
    match k {
        lab_primary => { return true },
        lab_secondary => { return false },
        lab_note => { return false },
        lab_help => { return false },
    }
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn is_note(k: LabelKind) -> bool {
    match k {
        lab_note => { return true },
        lab_primary => { return false },
        lab_secondary => { return false },
        lab_help => { return false },
    }
}

@no_alloc @no_os fn put_severity(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, sev: Severity) {
    match sev {
        sev_error => { sink.put_str(s, buf, "error") },
        sev_warning => { sink.put_str(s, buf, "warning") },
        sev_note => { sink.put_str(s, buf, "note") },
        sev_help => { sink.put_str(s, buf, "help") },
    }
}

// One numbered source row, tabs expanded.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_source_row(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read m: SourceMap, read th: Theme, w: usize, f: FileId, line: usize) {
    let src: str = line_text(m, f, line)
    put_head_num(s, buf, th, w, line)
    put_source(s, buf, src, th)
    sink.put_nl(s, buf)
}

// One underline row beneath the source row already emitted for `line`. It re-reads that
// line rather than taking its text as a parameter, because the caret's offset must be
// computed with the SAME tab expansion the source row used and the only way to guarantee
// that is to compute both from the one string.
@no_alloc @no_os fn put_caret_row(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read m: SourceMap, read th: Theme, w: usize, f: FileId, line: usize, col: usize, width: usize, under: i32, read text: str) {
    let src: str = line_text(m, f, line)
    put_head_bar(s, buf, th, w)
    sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
    put_pad(s, buf, expanded_width(src, col - 1, th))
    for i in 0..width {
        sink.put(s, buf, under as u8)
    }
    if text.len > 0 {
        sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
        sink.put_str(s, buf, text)
    }
    sink.put_nl(s, buf)
}

// Render `d` into `s`. Returns the number of bytes WRITTEN — which is not the number of
// bytes the diagnostic needs if the buffer was too small. **Check `sink.overflowed(s)`**;
// the header says why in full.
//
// Multi-line spans underline only their FIRST line, from the start column to the end of
// that line. Underlining a whole region takes a second rendering mode (rustc's `/` `|`
// `\` gutter), and a v1 that drew a caret run across a line break would place the run in
// the wrong place rather than in a simplified place. The label text still says what the
// region is.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn render(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read m: SourceMap, read d: Diagnostic, read th: Theme) -> usize {
    let before: usize = sink.written(s)

    // ---- the header: `error[E0042]: no field `z` on struct `Point``
    put_severity(s, buf, d.sev)
    let c: str = code(d)
    if c.len > 0 {
        sink.put(s, buf, 91 as u8)          // '['
        sink.put_str(s, buf, c)
        sink.put(s, buf, 93 as u8)          // ']'
    }
    sink.put(s, buf, 58 as u8)              // ':'
    sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
    sink.put_str(s, buf, message(d))
    sink.put_nl(s, buf)

    let n: usize = label_count(d)

    // ---- the gutter width: the widest line number any row will print
    var maxline: usize = 1
    var gi: usize = 0
    for gi < n {
        let l: Label = label_at(d, gi)
        if l.spanned {
            if has_file(m, l.span.file) {
                let p: Pos = pos_of(m, l.span.file, l.span.start)
                let last: usize = p.line + th.context_after
                if last > maxline { maxline = last }
            }
        }
        gi = gi + 1
    }
    var w: usize = digits(maxline)
    if th.gutter == false { w = 0 }

    // ---- the snippet block, GROUPED BY FILE
    //
    // Labels keep the caller's order, but the block is walked file by file: the outer
    // loop picks each file the first time a label mentions it, the inner loop then emits
    // every label in THAT file. Rendering strictly in label order instead is what the
    // first version did, and with a primary in `a.jtr`, a secondary in `b.jtr` and a
    // suggestion back in `a.jtr` it printed `a.jtr`'s source line twice under two
    // separate `-->` headers — which reads as two different places in the file. Grouping
    // costs one O(labels²) scan over a list that is almost always under five entries.
    //
    // Within a file, consecutive labels on the SAME line share one source row and stack
    // their underlines, which is what makes "here, and also here" legible.
    var any: bool = false
    var fi: usize = 0
    for fi < n {
        let lf: Label = label_at(d, fi)
        if lf.spanned == false or has_file(m, lf.span.file) == false {
            fi = fi + 1
            continue
        }
        // Already rendered under an earlier label's header?
        var seen: bool = false
        var q: usize = 0
        for q < fi {
            let lq: Label = label_at(d, q)
            if lq.spanned and has_file(m, lq.span.file) and lq.span.file == lf.span.file {
                seen = true
            }
            q = q + 1
        }
        if seen {
            fi = fi + 1
            continue
        }

        let f: FileId = lf.span.file
        let hp: Pos = pos_of(m, f, lf.span.start)
        put_pad(s, buf, w + 1)
        sink.put_str(s, buf, "--> ")
        sink.put_str(s, buf, file_name(m, f))
        sink.put(s, buf, 58 as u8)
        sink.put_usize(s, buf, hp.line)
        sink.put(s, buf, 58 as u8)
        sink.put_usize(s, buf, hp.col)
        sink.put_nl(s, buf)
        put_head_bar(s, buf, th, w)
        sink.put_nl(s, buf)

        let lc: usize = line_count(m, f)
        var last_line: usize = 0
        var li: usize = fi
        for li < n {
            let l: Label = label_at(d, li)
            if l.spanned == false or has_file(m, l.span.file) == false or l.span.file != f {
                li = li + 1
                continue
            }
            let p: Pos = pos_of(m, f, l.span.start)
            if p.line != last_line {
                // Context before — never re-printing a line this block already showed.
                var cb: usize = th.context_before
                if cb >= p.line { cb = p.line - 1 }
                var k: usize = p.line - cb
                if last_line > 0 and k <= last_line { k = last_line + 1 }
                for k < p.line {
                    put_source_row(s, buf, m, th, w, f, k)
                    k = k + 1
                }
                put_source_row(s, buf, m, th, w, f, p.line)
                last_line = p.line
            }
            // The underline. `width` is the span clamped to this line, and never zero:
            // a zero-width span (an insertion point) still has to point somewhere.
            let lt: str = line_text(m, f, p.line)
            var endc: usize = l.span.end
            if endc < l.span.start { endc = l.span.start }
            var nbytes: usize = endc - l.span.start
            let room: usize = lt.len + 1 - p.col
            if nbytes > room { nbytes = room }
            // The span is BYTES; the underline is CELLS. Measured as the difference of two
            // `expanded_width` calls rather than by measuring the slice, so the underline and
            // the indent above it are computed by one function over one string and cannot
            // disagree about a tab or a wide character — the same argument `put_source` makes
            // for sharing its expansion with the caret row.
            let lead: usize = expanded_width(lt, p.col - 1, th)
            var width: usize = expanded_width(lt, p.col - 1 + nbytes, th) - lead
            if width == 0 { width = 1 }
            put_caret_row(s, buf, m, th, w, f, p.line, p.col, width, under_char(l.kind), label_text(d, l))
            li = li + 1
        }
        // Context after the last labelled line in this file.
        var j: usize = last_line + 1
        let lim: usize = last_line + th.context_after
        for j <= lim {
            if j <= lc {
                put_source_row(s, buf, m, th, w, f, j)
            }
            j = j + 1
        }
        any = true
        fi = fi + 1
    }

    // ---- trailing `= note:` / `= help:` lines
    var ni: usize = 0
    var first_note: bool = true
    for ni < n {
        let l: Label = label_at(d, ni)
        if l.spanned == false {
            if first_note and any {
                put_head_bar(s, buf, th, w)
                sink.put_nl(s, buf)
                first_note = false
            }
            put_pad(s, buf, w + 2)
            sink.put(s, buf, EQ as u8)
            sink.put(s, buf, SP as u8)
            if is_note(l.kind) {
                sink.put_str(s, buf, "note: ")
            } else {
                sink.put_str(s, buf, "help: ")
            }
            sink.put_str(s, buf, label_text(d, l))
            sink.put_nl(s, buf)
        }
        ni = ni + 1
    }

    return sink.written(s) - before
}

// The one-line form: `file:line:col: error: message`, the shape a driver already prints
// and an editor already parses. No source, no caret, no allocation — for a `--brief` mode
// or for the second and subsequent occurrences of a repeated diagnostic.
//
// It exists so that "structured" does not become a synonym for "verbose": the DATA is the
// structure, and how much of it a caller renders is the caller's policy. A tool that had
// to build a different diagnostic type to get a short line would rebuild the whole thing.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn render_brief(mut s: Sink, mut buf: []u8, read m: SourceMap, read d: Diagnostic) -> usize {
    let before: usize = sink.written(s)
    let n: usize = label_count(d)
    var found: bool = false
    var li: usize = 0
    for li < n {
        let l: Label = label_at(d, li)
        if l.spanned and is_primary(l.kind) and found == false {
            if has_file(m, l.span.file) {
                let p: Pos = pos_of(m, l.span.file, l.span.start)
                sink.put_str(s, buf, file_name(m, l.span.file))
                sink.put(s, buf, 58 as u8)
                sink.put_usize(s, buf, p.line)
                sink.put(s, buf, 58 as u8)
                sink.put_usize(s, buf, p.col)
                sink.put_str(s, buf, ": ")
                found = true
            }
        }
        li = li + 1
    }
    put_severity(s, buf, d.sev)
    let c: str = code(d)
    if c.len > 0 {
        sink.put(s, buf, 91 as u8)
        sink.put_str(s, buf, c)
        sink.put(s, buf, 93 as u8)
    }
    sink.put_str(s, buf, ": ")
    sink.put_str(s, buf, message(d))
    return sink.written(s) - before
}
